WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.312
MERGE is an optional advanced topic so do not worry
if this

00:00:03.338 --> 00:00:07.662
feels more complex than the earlier commands MERGE
is useful when you

00:00:07.688 --> 00:00:10.338
have a source dataset and a target table and you
want one

00:00:10.362 --> 00:00:14.250
statement to decide what should happen for each
source row The most

00:00:14.275 --> 00:00:18.462
important part is the ON condition The ON condition
defines when a

00:00:18.488 --> 00:00:22.538
source row and a target row represent the same
record In this

00:00:22.562 --> 00:00:27.188
example the match is based on MRN When a source MRN
already

00:00:27.212 --> 00:00:31.875
exists in the target the WHEN MATCHED branch updates
selected fields When

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:35.362
a source MRN does not exist the WHEN NOT MATCHED
branch inserts

00:00:35.388 --> 00:00:40.862
a new row The example first creates a temporary
incoming_patients table adds

00:00:40.875 --> 00:00:44.200
a couple of source rows and then merges that source
into patients

00:00:45.075 --> 00:00:50.262
After the MERGE a SELECT shows the relevant records
If your PostgreSQL

00:00:50.288 --> 00:00:54.088
environment supports MERGE you can run the example
and compare the matched

00:00:54.112 --> 00:00:58.412
row with the newly inserted row The safety lesson is
more important

00:00:58.438 --> 00:01:02.850
than the syntax Before using MERGE on important data
make sure the

00:01:02.875 --> 00:01:06.500
source does not contain unexpected duplicates and
make sure the ON condition

00:01:06.525 --> 00:01:10.825
really identifies the same entity A poor match rule
can update the

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:12.725
wrong records or create duplicates
